=begin pod

=TITLE App::FontSample

=SUBTITLE Create PDF font samples from C<PDF::Content::FontObj> objects

=head1 DESCRIPTION

C<App::FontSample> creates PDF font specimens, comparisons, compact font
collections, and character charts.

C<App::FontSample> does not provide a font collection. Library callers supply
one or more C<PDF::Content::FontObj> objects. The installed C<font-sample>
program can load an OTF or TTF file directly.

The current release supports Letter and A4 paper, portrait and landscape
orientation, configurable margins, several sample layouts, JSON job files,
and registered language pangrams.

=head1 INSTALLATION

=begin code :lang<text>
zef install App::FontSample
=end code

=head1 FIRST SAMPLE

The easiest first test uses a standard PDF core font, so no external font
file is required.

=begin code :lang<raku>
use PDF::API6;
use App::FontSample;

my $pdf = PDF::API6.new;
my $font = $pdf.core-font: :family<Times-Roman>;

create-font-sample(
    $font,
    :name<Times-Roman>,
    :output<sample.pdf>,
);
=end code

This creates C<sample.pdf> using the default C<specimen> layout.

=head1 LIBRARY INTERFACE

=head2 create-font-sample

C<create-font-sample> creates a sample from one
C<PDF::Content::FontObj>.

=begin code :lang<raku>
create-font-sample(
    $font,
    :name<MyFont-Regular>,
    :paper<Letter>,
    :layout<specimen>,
    :output<my-font.pdf>,
);
=end code

Current common named options are:

=item C<name> - displayed font name; required
=item C<output> - output PDF pathname; required
=item C<paper> - C<Letter> or C<A4>; default C<Letter>
=item C<media> - alternate name for C<paper>
=item C<landscape> - use landscape orientation
=item C<margin> - page margin in PDF points; default 36
=item C<layout> - layout name; default C<specimen>
=item C<title> - document title
=item C<family> - optional family description
=item C<style> - optional style description
=item C<language> - registered language code used to select a pangram
=item C<debug> - enable diagnostic messages

PDF dimensions are expressed in points; 72 points equal one inch.

=head2 create-font-collection-sample

C<create-font-collection-sample> accepts a non-empty collection of
C<App::FontSample::FontEntry> objects. Its default layout is C<comparison>.

=begin code :lang<raku>
use App::FontSample;
use App::FontSample::FontEntry;

my @entries;

@entries.push: App::FontSample::FontEntry.new(
    :name<Font-One>,
    :font($font-one),
);

@entries.push: App::FontSample::FontEntry.new(
    :name<Font-Two>,
    :font($font-two),
);

create-font-collection-sample(
    @entries,
    :layout<comparison>,
    :output<comparison.pdf>,
);
=end code

=head1 CURRENT LAYOUTS

=head2 specimen

The C<specimen> layout produces one specimen page per font. It shows the
font name, uppercase and lowercase alphabets, numerals and punctuation, a
pangram, and a size waterfall.

=begin code :lang<raku>
create-font-sample(
    $font,
    :name<Times-Roman>,
    :layout<specimen>,
    :output<specimen.pdf>,
);
=end code

The pangram and waterfall text may be replaced:

=begin code :lang<raku>
create-font-sample(
    $font,
    :name<Times-Roman>,
    :layout<specimen>,
    :pangram('Pack my box with five dozen liquor jugs.'),
    :text('Sphinx of black quartz, judge my vow.'),
    :sizes(8, 10, 12, 14, 18, 24, 36),
    :output<specimen-custom.pdf>,
);
=end code

=head2 waterfall

C<waterfall> is currently an alternate layout name for the specimen
renderer. Consequently it produces the specimen information as well as the
size waterfall. C<sizes> controls the waterfall sizes.

=begin code :lang<raku>
create-font-sample(
    $font,
    :name<Times-Roman>,
    :layout<waterfall>,
    :text('Sphinx of black quartz, judge my vow. 0123456789'),
    :sizes(8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 14, 18, 24, 30, 36, 48, 60),
    :output<waterfall.pdf>,
);
=end code

=head2 comparison

C<comparison> displays the same sample text in each supplied font.
C<comparison-size> defaults to 18 points. The layout automatically continues
onto additional pages when the rows do not fit on one page.

=begin code :lang<raku>
create-font-collection-sample(
    @entries,
    :layout<comparison>,
    :comparison-size(18),
    :text('Hamburgefonts 0123456789 Aa Bb Cc'),
    :output<comparison.pdf>,
);
=end code

=head2 collection

C<collection> places a compact collection of fonts on one page.
C<sample-size> defaults to 14 points and C<leading-ratio> defaults to 0.20.

=begin code :lang<raku>
create-font-collection-sample(
    @entries,
    :layout<collection>,
    :sample-size(14),
    :leading-ratio(0.20),
    :text('Hamburgefonts 0123456789 Aa Bb Cc'),
    :output<collection.pdf>,
);
=end code

The collection layout reports an error rather than allowing the rows to
overflow the lower page margin. Use C<comparison> when the collection is too
large for one page.

=head2 characters

C<characters> displays selected characters in a grid with a C<U+XXXX> label
beneath each glyph. C<columns> defaults to 8 and the library C<glyph-size>
option defaults to 30 points. The layout automatically creates additional
pages when necessary.

=begin code :lang<raku>
create-font-sample(
    $font,
    :name<Times-Roman>,
    :layout<characters>,
    :characters('ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789'),
    :columns(8),
    :glyph-size(30),
    :output<characters.pdf>,
);
=end code

Library callers may supply C<characters> as a string or as a positional
collection containing individual characters, integer Unicode code points,
or strings of the form C<U+XXXX>.

=begin code :lang<raku>
my @characters =
    'A',
    'B',
    0x43,
    'U+0044';

create-font-sample(
    $font,
    :name<Times-Roman>,
    :layout<characters>,
    :characters(@characters),
    :output<selected-characters.pdf>,
);
=end code

=head1 PAPER AND PAGE OPTIONS

Both C<Letter> and C<A4> are supported.

=begin code :lang<raku>
create-font-sample(
    $font,
    :name<Times-Roman>,
    :paper<A4>,
    :landscape,
    :margin(36),
    :output<a4-landscape.pdf>,
);
=end code

=head1 LANGUAGE PANGRAMS

C<App::FontSample::SampleText> contains a small registry of language
pangrams. A registered language code can supply the specimen pangram:

=begin code :lang<raku>
create-font-sample(
    $font,
    :name<Times-Roman>,
    :language<en>,
    :output<english.pdf>,
);
=end code

The installed program lists the currently registered language codes:

=begin code :lang<text>
font-sample --languages
=end code

A library caller may also register additional sample text at runtime.

=head1 USING AN OTF OR TTF FILE

The installed C<font-sample> program loads an OTF or TTF file directly.

=begin code :lang<text>
font-sample \
    --font=/path/to/font.otf \
    --output=sample.pdf \
    --layout=specimen \
    --paper=Letter
=end code

Current direct-mode options are:

=item C<--name=TEXT>
=item C<--family=TEXT>
=item C<--style=TEXT>
=item C<--layout=specimen|waterfall|comparison|characters|collection>
=item C<--paper=Letter|A4>
=item C<--landscape>
=item C<--margin=POINTS>
=item C<--language=CODE>
=item C<--text=TEXT>
=item C<--pangram=TEXT>
=item C<--sizes=N,N,...>
=item C<--characters=TEXT>
=item C<--columns=N>
=item C<--comparison-size=N>
=item C<--sample-size=N>
=item C<--leading-ratio=N>
=item C<--languages>
=item C<--help>

For example:

=begin code :lang<text>
font-sample \
    --font=/path/to/font.otf \
    --output=characters.pdf \
    --layout=characters \
    --characters=ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789 \
    --columns=8
=end code

=head1 JSON INPUT

C<font-sample> can read one or more jobs from a JSON definition file:

=begin code :lang<text>
font-sample --config=font-samples.json
=end code

A single job can be defined as:

=begin code :lang<json>
{
    "output": "output/sample.pdf",
    "layout": "specimen",
    "fonts": [
        {
            "file": "fonts/MyFont.otf",
            "name": "My Font"
        }
    ]
}
=end code

Several related jobs may share defaults:

=begin code :lang<json>
{
    "defaults": {
        "paper": "Letter",
        "margin": 36,
        "fonts": [
            {
                "file": "fonts/MyFont-Regular.otf",
                "name": "MyFont Regular"
            },
            {
                "file": "fonts/MyFont-Bold.otf",
                "name": "MyFont Bold"
            }
        ]
    },
    "samples": [
        {
            "output": "output/comparison.pdf",
            "layout": "comparison",
            "comparison-size": 18
        },
        {
            "output": "output/characters.pdf",
            "layout": "characters",
            "characters": "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789",
            "columns": 8
        }
    ]
}
=end code

Values in an individual sample override values in C<defaults>. Relative font
and output paths are interpreted relative to the JSON definition file.

=head1 OPTIONAL NOTOFONTS-OT USE

C<NotoFonts-OT> is not a dependency of C<App::FontSample>. It is one
optional source of C<PDF::Content::FontObj> objects.

=begin code :lang<raku>
use NotoFonts-OT;
use App::FontSample;
use App::FontSample::FontEntry;

my @entries;

for <
    NotoSerif-Regular
    NotoSerif-Bold
    NotoSans-Regular
    NotoSans-Bold
> -> $code {
    my $font = get-loaded-font($code);

    @entries.push: App::FontSample::FontEntry.new(
        :name($code),
        :$font,
    );
}

create-font-collection-sample(
    @entries,
    :layout<comparison>,
    :output<noto-comparison.pdf>,
);
=end code

=head1 EXAMPLES

The C<examples> directory contains programs demonstrating current
capabilities. C<examples/current-capabilities.raku> uses PDF core fonts and
creates examples of every current layout.

The Noto examples demonstrate optional integration with C<NotoFonts-OT>.

=head1 TESTING

Run the normal test suite with:

=begin code :lang<text>
zef test .
=end code

The normal test suite does not require C<NotoFonts-OT>. Noto integration is
tested separately so the core application remains independent of a
particular font collection.

=head1 FUTURE WORK

This README describes current capability only. Planned additions and changes
are maintained in C<docs/TODO.rakudoc>.

=head1 SOURCE

The project source is maintained in the C<tbrowder/App-FontSample> repository
on GitHub.

=head1 AUTHOR

